    written: [
      { q: "Name two ancient towns of India.", a: "Any two: Varanasi, Prayag (Allahabad), Pataliputra (Patna), Madurai.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is a mega city?", a: "A city with a population of more than 5 million.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two physical factors that affect the types of rural settlements.", a: "1. Terrain and altitude (plains encourage compact villages; fragmented hills lead to scattered ones).\n2. Climate and availability of water.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Differentiate between clustered and dispersed rural settlements.", a: "1. Clustered: a compact, closely built-up area of houses with distinct streets, found in fertile alluvial plains and the north-eastern states.\n2. Dispersed: isolated huts or small hamlets spread over fragmented terrain such as hills and forests, as in Meghalaya, Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh and Kerala.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the hamleted settlement and say where it is found.", a: "1. It is a settlement fragmented into several physically separated units that share a common name.\n2. The units are locally called panna, para, palli, nagla or dhani.\n3. It is found in the middle and lower Ganga plain, Chhattisgarh and the lower valleys of the Himalaya.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the functional classification of towns with two examples each of administrative, mining and educational towns.", a: "1. Towns are classified by the main function they perform, though many become multi-functional.\n2. Administrative: Chandigarh, Shillong, Bhopal. Mining: Raniganj, Jharia, Digboi.\n3. Educational: Roorkee, Pilani, Aligarh, Varanasi.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What are the aims of the Smart Cities Mission?", a: "1. To promote cities that provide core infrastructure and a decent quality of life.\n2. To ensure a clean and sustainable environment using smart solutions such as technology and data.\n3. To promote sustainable and inclusive development that can be replicated in other areas.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the types of rural settlements in India with the areas where they are found.", a: "1. Clustered (nucleated): compact houses; fertile alluvial plains, north-east, Bundelkhand, Nagaland (security) and Rajasthan (scarce water).\n2. Semi-clustered: a dominant community in the centre and lower strata on the outer flanks; Gujarat plain and parts of Rajasthan.\n3. Hamleted: separated units with a common name; middle and lower Ganga plain, Chhattisgarh and lower Himalayan valleys.\n4. Dispersed: isolated huts in remote hills and jungles; Meghalaya, Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh and Kerala.\n5. Types depend on physical features, cultural and ethnic factors, and security factors.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Trace the evolution of towns in India from ancient to modern times.", a: "1. Ancient towns (over 2,000 years old) were religious and cultural centres: Varanasi, Prayag, Pataliputra, Madurai.\n2. Medieval towns were headquarters of principalities and fort towns: Delhi, Hyderabad, Jaipur, Lucknow, Agra, Nagpur.\n3. Modern towns began as European trading ports (Surat, Daman, Goa, Pondicherry), British centres (Mumbai, Chennai, Kolkata), hill stations and industrial towns like Jamshedpur.\n4. After Independence came administrative centres (Chandigarh, Bhubaneswar, Gandhinagar, Dispur) and industrial towns (Durgapur, Bhilai, Sindri, Barauni).\n5. Satellite towns such as Ghaziabad, Rohtak and Gurugram grew around metropolitan cities.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      {
        q: "Case study (invented figures): Town Y has a population of 7,500 and a density of 520 persons per sq km. It has 4,800 male main workers, of whom 3,720 work in non-agricultural activities. It has no municipality.\n(i) Calculate the percentage of male main workers in non-agricultural work.\n(ii) Does Town Y meet the Census criteria for an urban area?\n(iii) Is it a Class I town? Give a reason.",
        a: "(i) 3,720 ÷ 4,800 × 100 = 77.5%.\n(ii) Yes. Population 7,500 is at least 5,000, density 520 is at least 400 and 77.5% is at least 75%, so it qualifies as a census town.\n(iii) No. A Class I town needs a population of 1 lakh or more, and Town Y has only 7,500.",
        marks: 4, type: "case"
      }
    ]
